var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Ephemeris-Files","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"An ephemeris describes an object's position and velocity over time. Use read_ephemeris to load an ephemeris into Epicycle, and write_ephemeris to export a propagated trajectory or convert an existing file to another supported format.\n\nEphemerides are represented as SpacecraftHistory objects, the same history type used to record a spacecraft's propagation. A history contains one or more segments, each with its own epochs, states, coordinate system, and metadata. Segment boundaries preserve discontinuities such as impulsive maneuvers.","title":"Ephemeris Files"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Supported-formats","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"The filename extension selects the format. Use the format keyword when a filename has a different extension or no extension.\n\nFormat Extension Explicit selection Support\nCCSDS Orbit Ephemeris Message (OEM) .oem OEM() Read versions 1.0–3.0 in keyword/value form; write 3.0 or 2.0\nSTK ephemeris .e STKEphemeris() Read Cartesian position, position/velocity, and position/velocity/acceleration data; write Cartesian ephemerides\nJPL SPK .bsp SPK() Read stored samples or sample a segment through SPICE; write type 9 (Lagrange) or type 13 (Hermite)","title":"Supported formats"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Read-an-ephemeris","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"using Epicycle\n\neph = read_ephemeris(\"trajectory.oem\")\n\n# Specify the format if the extension does not identify it.\neph = read_ephemeris(\"trajectory.txt\"; format = OEM())\n\nEach file segment becomes a history segment in the coordinate system specified by the file. Positions and velocities are represented in km and km/s, regardless of the file's distance units. Accelerations, when present, use km/s²; covariance entries use the corresponding products of these units.","title":"Read an ephemeris"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Inspect-the-history","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"sample_times = epochs(eph)\nt_start, t_stop = span(eph)\nintervals = coverage(eph)\nread_notes = diagnostics(eph)\n\nepochs returns the stored sample epochs. span returns the beginning and end of the overall coverage, which may contain gaps. Use coverage to find the intervals where the history can be evaluated: it respects each segment's usable span and merges intervals that touch or overlap.\n\nEach segment's metadata retains the object's name and ID, the declared interpolation method, and any accelerations, covariance records, or usable span, under the keys \"object_name\", \"object_id\", \"interpolation\", \"accelerations\", \"covariances\", \"useable_start\" and \"useable_stop\". The \"source\" entry records file details and read diagnostics.","title":"Inspect the history"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Read-an-SPK-file","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"Select an object with naif_id if the SPK contains more than one object:\n\neph = read_ephemeris(\"mission.bsp\"; naif_id = -900001)\n\nSPK types 9 and 13 are read using their stored samples, epochs, and interpolation degree. For other supported types, including Chebyshev segments, provide a sampling interval in seconds:\n\neph = read_ephemeris(\"mission.bsp\"; naif_id = -900001, step = 60.0)\ndiagnostics(eph)\n\nThe reader evaluates these segments through SPICE and builds a sampled history. It also compares the history's interpolation with SPICE at the midpoints between samples and records the largest position difference in the diagnostics. Use this estimate to assess the sampling interval; it is not a bound on the error at every time. The read step keyword does not resample type 9 or type 13 segments.","title":"Read an SPK file"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Evaluate-and-compare-trajectories","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"state_at returns a Coordinate containing the interpolated state, its coordinate system, and its epoch:\n\nt = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:30:30\", UTC(), ISOT())\nc = state_at(eph, t)\nx = to_vector(CartesianState(c))  # [x, y, z, vx, vy, vz], in km and km/s\n\nThe query time must fall within the history's coverage. Interpolation uses the segment's declared method, or Lagrange(7) when no method is specified (lower for a segment of fewer than eight samples), and never crosses a segment boundary. Times in a gap or outside the coverage raise an error; Epicycle does not extrapolate.\n\nAt a shared segment endpoint, the later segment supplies the state, so a query at an impulsive maneuver returns the state after the event. If usable spans overlap beyond a shared endpoint, select a segment explicitly:\n\nc = state_at(eph, t; overlap = :later)  # or :earlier\n\nUse position_difference to compare trajectories. It evaluates the second trajectory at each of the first trajectory's sample epochs that the second covers, converts it to the first's coordinate system, and returns the distances in km.\n\nerrors_km = position_difference(eph, sat)\nmaximum(errors_km)","title":"Evaluate and compare trajectories"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Write-an-ephemeris","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"write_ephemeris accepts a Spacecraft or a SpacecraftHistory and returns the output path. For a spacecraft, it writes the recorded history; propagate the spacecraft before exporting it. An existing output file is replaced.\n\nThe following example propagates a spacecraft for one hour and exports an OEM with samples at 60-second intervals:\n\nusing Epicycle\n\nepoch = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0.0, 1300.0, 0.0, 7.35, 1.0]),\n                 time = epoch,\n                 name = \"LEO\")\nforces = ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ()))\ninteg = IntegratorConfig(DP8(); reltol = 1e-12, abstol = 1e-12)\npropagate!(OrbitPropagator(forces, integ), sat,\n           StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 3600.0))\n\noutput_dir = mktempdir()\npath = joinpath(output_dir, \"leo.oem\")\nwrite_ephemeris(path, sat; step = 60.0)\n\neph = read_ephemeris(path)\nlength(epochs(eph))\n61","title":"Write an ephemeris"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Sampling,-interpolation,-and-coordinates","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"By default, the writer keeps the source epochs. Set step to resample each segment at a fixed interval in seconds, including its first and last epochs. The last interval may be shorter than step.\n\nThe interpolation keyword specifies the interpolation method declared in the output file. It defaults to the source's method, or Lagrange(7) if none is recorded. Resampling evaluates the source using its existing interpolation method; setting interpolation alone does not create new samples. Each segment must contain enough samples for the selected method.\n\nFor OEM and STK, the writer uses each segment's coordinate system unless you supply coordinate_system, in which case it converts the states before writing. SPK is always written in ICRF: leave coordinate_system out and each segment is rotated to ICRF; any other choice is refused. OEM epochs default to UTC; use time_system to select another supported time system. STK times are UTC-based, and SPK uses TDB ephemeris time.\n\nwrite_ephemeris(\"trajectory.oem\", sat;\n                step = 60.0,\n                interpolation = Hermite(7),\n                coordinate_system = EarthMJ2000Eq,\n                time_system = UTC())\n\nSegment boundaries are preserved. A redundant one-state segment recorded by a maneuver is omitted when its state matches a neighboring segment's endpoint. Other isolated one-state segments cannot be exported because they cannot be interpolated.","title":"Sampling, interpolation, and coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Convert-between-formats","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"Read a file, then write the resulting history with the desired extension:\n\nstk_path = joinpath(output_dir, \"leo.e\")\nwrite_ephemeris(stk_path, eph; interpolation = Hermite(7))\n\nspk_path = joinpath(output_dir, \"leo.bsp\")\nwrite_ephemeris(spk_path, eph; naif_id = -900001)\n\nmaximum(position_difference(read_ephemeris(spk_path), sat))\n4.5778249012391313e-7\n\nConversion preserves supported trajectory data, but formats differ in what they can represent. In particular:\n\nOEM: The writer defaults to version 3.0. Use version = \"2.0\" for tools that require it. message_id and classification require version 3.0. A new file does not reuse the source message ID. The source classification marking is retained unless explicitly overridden.\nSTK: Segments must share their endpoints; this format cannot represent gaps between segments. The writer explicitly records the distance unit and converts the interpolation degree to STK's InterpolationSamplesM1 convention. STK has no usable span, so a segment usable over less than its data is refused unless you pass discard_unsupported = true; the file then reads back with its whole data span as coverage. All segments must share one central body, frame, time system and interpolation method. STK output supports EME2000, ICRF, and Earth's ITRF, TOD, MOD and TEME; use coordinate_system to convert other frames.\nSPK: Supply the object's naif_id when writing. Hermite interpolation produces type 13 segments; Lagrange produces type 9. SPK output is always in ICRF axes (called J2000 by SPICE). States in any other frame, EME2000 and J2000ECLIPTIC included, are rotated to ICRF rather than relabeled, and an ECLIPJ2000 segment read from a file is rotated to ICRF the same way. SPK's 64-bit ephemeris time has a resolution of approximately 0.1 µs in this century. The writer refuses position-only data, a `naifid` that belongs to a natural body, and segments whose usable spans overlap; the object name becomes the SPK segment name, at most 40 printable ASCII characters. The reader takes J2000 and ECLIPJ2000 segments, and a segment sampled through SPICE needs at least eight samples.","title":"Convert between formats"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Accelerations,-covariance,-and-metadata","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"Accelerations and covariance are written where the output format supports them. The writer rejects resampling with step when the source contains these data. It also rejects a change of coordinate system for such a source, because that conversion transforms states only.\n\nSPK cannot carry accelerations or covariance, and STK cannot carry a usable span. To explicitly omit those data, use discard_unsupported = true. For SPK they are dropped before the states are resampled or rotated to ICRF; without the option, writing such a source to SPK is refused.\n\nSTK and SPK cannot carry a classification marking. Exporting a marked history to either format requires an explicit classification = \"\".\n\nSet object_name and object_id to identify the object (in SPK, the name becomes the segment name), and, for OEM, originator to identify the file producer. Object names and IDs otherwise come from source metadata where available; a spacecraft's name is used as a fallback object name, and missing identifiers are written as \"UNKNOWN\". The default originator is \"EPICYCLE\".\n\nFor OEM, digits sets the decimal places of seconds in every epoch, from 0 to 9, default 9. For STK it sets only the ScenarioEpoch; data times are measured from it, so nothing shifts. It does not control state precision; OEM state values use 16 significant digits. A rounded OEM epoch keeps its state, so rounding would shift the trajectory in time; the writer refuses a digits setting that moves any epoch by more than 1 ns. Epochs on whole seconds can be written with digits = 0, while those that are not, such as a UTC grid written in TDB, need the default.\n\nThe writer validates the output before opening the file. Validation failures raise an ArgumentError and leave an existing file unchanged.","title":"Accelerations, covariance, and metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Handle-read-problems","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"By default, readers correct supported, recoverable deviations, such as lowercase keywords or exact duplicate records, and issue a combined warning. Assumptions, such as an explicit metadata override, are recorded as notes. Inspect both with diagnostics(eph) after a successful read.\n\nProblems that prevent a read are collected in an EphemerisReadError. For text formats, the error identifies the affected lines and fields so you can locate the problem in the file.\n\nOption Use\nstrict = true Reject recoverable deviations as errors instead of correcting them.\nskip_bad_lines = true For OEM and STK, drop unreadable data rows with a warning and split the segment at each omission. Each remaining piece must have enough samples for interpolation.\nref_frame, center For OEM and STK, override the declared frame or central body.\ntime_system For OEM only, override the declared time system.\n\nOverrides change how the reader interprets the numbers; they do not transform the states. Use them only when you know the file's declaration is incorrect or uses a name Epicycle does not recognize. To transform correctly labeled data, use the writer's coordinate_system option instead.\n\neph = read_ephemeris(\"vendor.oem\"; ref_frame = \"EME2000\", center = \"EARTH\")\ndiagnostics(eph)\n\nSPK identifies frames and centers by NAIF codes and does not accept these overrides. STK does not accept a time-system override because its times are UTC-based.","title":"Handle read problems"},{"category":"section","location":"ephemeris/#Frames,-centers,-and-time-systems","page":"Ephemeris files","text":"Supported file frame names include EME2000 (also J2000 in OEM), ICRF, GCRF, ITRF and its realizations, TEME, TOD, MODEARTH, J2000ECLIPTIC, CIRS, TIRS, GTOD, MOONME, MOONPA440, and body-fixed FIXED_CB. ITRF realizations map to Epicycle's ITRF, with a diagnostic note describing the assumption.\n\nSupported centers are the Sun, planets, Pluto, and Moon. Barycenters and libration points are not supported as Epicycle origins. OEM time systems include UTC, TAI, TT, TDB, TCB, TCG, and GPS; ET is interpreted as TDB.\n\nSTK frame names include J2000, ICRF, Fixed, TrueOfDate, MeanOfDate, and TEMEOfDate about Earth. STK's Inertial frame depends on the central body and STK version, and the Moon's Fixed frame is ambiguous. For those cases, the reader requires an explicit ref_frame instead of choosing a frame automatically.\n\nSee the API reference for complete keyword descriptions.","title":"Frames, centers, and time systems"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Data-plotting","page":"Data plotting","text":"EpicycleIO displays interactive data plots in a browser dashboard. Use it to inspect time histories, compare results, explore distributions, and visualize scalar fields. The plotting functions accept arrays, including data extracted from a spacecraft's recorded history.\n\nA plot contains one or more traces: a line, a set of points, a bar series, or another representation of data. Traces appear in named panels. Supply trace appearance settings to the plotting function and panel settings, such as axis labels and legend position, to panel!.\n\nData plots use Plotly. Attribute names follow Plotly's naming, with underscores for nested properties: line_color sets a line's color and xaxis_title sets an axis title. You do not need to construct a Plotly figure to get started.","title":"Data plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Create-your-first-plot","page":"Data plotting","text":"This example compares two synthetic altitude histories:\n\nusing EpicycleIO\n\nhours = collect(0.0:0.1:6.0)\naltitude_a = 400.0 .+ 20.0 .* sin.(hours)\naltitude_b = 430.0 .+ 15.0 .* sin.(hours .+ 0.5)\n\nxyplot(\"Altitude\", hours, altitude_a;\n       mode = \"lines\", name = \"Spacecraft A\", line_width = 2)\nxyplot!(\"Altitude\", hours, altitude_b;\n        mode = \"lines\", name = \"Spacecraft B\", line_dash = \"dash\")\npanel!(\"Altitude\";\n       title = \"Altitude comparison\",\n       xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\",\n       yaxis_title = \"Altitude (km)\",\n       showlegend = true,\n       legend_orientation = \"h\")\n\nThe first plotting call opens the dashboard automatically. Hover over a plot to inspect values and use its browser controls to explore the data. See The dashboard for arranging panels and opening selected views.\n\nThe leading string identifies the panel. The name keyword labels a trace in its legend. Multiple named traces can share one panel.","title":"Create your first plot"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Plot-interfaces","page":"Data plotting","text":"Each convenience plotting function has a replacing form and an adding form:\n\nA call without ! replaces the panel's plotted data. Reusing a panel name lets you rerun a script without creating another panel.\nA call with ! adds traces while keeping existing traces. It does not append samples to an existing trace or change that trace's data.\nOmitting the panel name uses the default panel, \"Plot\".\nPlotting functions return nothing; their output appears on the dashboard.\n\nReplacing data resets the interactive view. Adding traces preserves the user's zoom. Layout settings survive data replacement, and successive panel! calls merge settings. You can configure a panel before plotting into it; check the spelling if panel! warns that a panel does not exist yet.","title":"Plot interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Accepted-data-shapes","page":"Data plotting","text":"The paired-series interface accepts the following forms:\n\nCall Interpretation\nxyplot(y) Values against sample indices 1:length(y).\nxyplot(x, y) One series; both arrays must have equal lengths.\nxyplot(x, Y) with a matrix One series per column; rows must match length(x).\nxyplot(x, samples) with a vector of vectors One trace per component when each outer entry is a sample.\nxyplot(x1, y1, x2, y2, ...) Independent pairs, each with its own length.\n\nA matrix supplied without x uses its row indices as horizontal coordinates. A vector of vectors can also contain one full-length vector per series. If both interpretations are possible, outer entries are treated as samples. Use a matrix with one column per series to make the orientation explicit.\n\nx = collect(0.0:0.25:4.0)\nY = hcat(sin.(x), cos.(x))\nxyplot(\"Signals\", x, Y;\n       mode = \"lines\", name = [\"Sine\", \"Cosine\"],\n       line_color = [\"cyan\", \"orange\"])\n\nThe paired-series rules also apply to bar, scatterpolar, and scattergeo. Grid plots, scatter3d, and band have distinct interfaces described below.","title":"Accepted data shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Units,-frames,-and-time","page":"Data plotting","text":"Data plots use the supplied values. They do not infer units, transform reference frames, or convert time systems. Convert data before plotting and include units in axis labels.\n\nConvert Epicycle Time objects before using them as coordinates. For example, after propagating a spacecraft named sat, extract its history and express elapsed time in hours:\n\nusing Epicycle\n\ntimes, positions = history(Calc(epoch, sat),\n                           Calc(position_vector, sat, EarthMJ2000Eq))\nhours = [(t - first(times)) * 24 for t in times]\nxyplot(\"Position\", hours, positions;\n       mode = \"lines\", name = [\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"])\npanel!(\"Position\";\n       xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\",\n       yaxis_title = \"Position in EarthMJ2000Eq (km)\")\n\nThe position history is a vector of component vectors, so this draws three traces. For a calendar axis, supply date strings and set xaxis_type = \"date\". NaN and Inf in real-valued series are rendered as gaps.","title":"Units, frames, and time"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Supported-plot-types","page":"Data plotting","text":"Every function below accepts an optional leading panel name and trace keywords. Each has an adding form, such as heatmap! or band!.\n\nFunction Data arguments Use\nxyplot y, x, y, or multiple pairs Cartesian lines, points, and combinations.\nbar categories, values Compare discrete categories.\nhistogram values Show a sample distribution.\nband x, lower, upper Shade an interval or uncertainty envelope.\ncontour x, y, Z or Z Show levels of a scalar field.\nheatmap x, y, Z or Z Show values as colored cells.\nscatterpolar angle, radius Plot angular and radial data.\nscattergeo longitude, latitude Plot geographic locations or tracks.\nscatter3d x, y, z Plot points or lines in three dimensions.\nsurface x, y, Z or Z Draw a scalar field as a 3D surface.","title":"Supported plot types"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Lines,-markers,-and-filled-curves","page":"Data plotting","text":"Use an explicit mode to choose the representation:\n\nx = collect(0.0:0.2:4.0)\ny = sin.(x)\n\nxyplot(\"Line\", x, y;\n       mode = \"lines\", name = \"Prediction\",\n       line_color = \"cyan\", line_width = 2, line_dash = \"dash\")\nxyplot(\"Points\", x, y;\n       mode = \"markers\", name = \"Measurements\",\n       marker_color = \"orange\", marker_size = 6, marker_symbol = \"circle\")\nxyplot(\"Line and points\", x, y;\n       mode = \"lines+markers\", name = \"Samples\")\nxyplot(\"Filled curve\", x, abs.(y);\n       mode = \"lines\", fill = \"tozeroy\",\n       fillcolor = \"rgba(0,220,255,0.2)\", name = \"Magnitude\")\n\nUse line_shape = \"hv\" for a step curve or line_shape = \"spline\" for a smooth curve. Choose interpolation that represents your data appropriately. Error bars can be specified with error_y_type = \"data\" and error_y_array = uncertainties, where uncertainties contains one magnitude per point.\n\nxyplot and xyplot! use WebGL for every trace in the call when any series in it exceeds 4,000 points. Rendering and supported attributes can differ from ordinary scatter; other plot types do not use this automatic switch.","title":"Lines, markers, and filled curves"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Bar-charts","page":"Data plotting","text":"bar(\"Maneuver budget\", [\"Departure\", \"Correction\", \"Arrival\"],\n    [2.82, 1.14, 0.48]; name = \"Nominal\", marker_color = \"cyan\")\nbar!(\"Maneuver budget\", [\"Departure\", \"Correction\", \"Arrival\"],\n     [2.95, 1.20, 0.51]; name = \"Reserve\", marker_color = \"orange\")\npanel!(\"Maneuver budget\";\n       barmode = \"group\", xaxis_title = \"Maneuver\", yaxis_title = \"Delta-V (km/s)\")\n\nUse barmode = \"stack\" to stack series. For horizontal bars, supply numerical values as the first coordinate and category labels as the second, with orientation = \"h\".","title":"Bar charts"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Histograms","page":"Data plotting","text":"residuals = [0.1, -0.2, 0.05, 0.3, -0.1, 0.08, 0.15, -0.12]\nhistogram(\"Residual distribution\", residuals;\n          name = \"Range\", xbins_size = 0.1, marker_color = \"cyan\")\npanel!(\"Residual distribution\";\n       xaxis_title = \"Range residual (km)\", yaxis_title = \"Count\")\n\nSupply samples rather than precomputed bin counts. Plotly chooses bins unless you configure them. nbinsx sets an upper bound on the number of bins; xbins_size sets the width. Use histnorm = \"probability\" or \"probability density\" for normalized displays and label the vertical axis accordingly. For overlapping distributions, add traces with histogram!, set panel barmode = \"overlay\", and adjust each trace's opacity.","title":"Histograms"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Uncertainty-bands","page":"Data plotting","text":"x = collect(0.0:0.1:4.0)\nprediction = sin.(x)\nsigma = fill(0.15, length(x))\n\nband(\"Envelope\", x, prediction .- 3 .* sigma, prediction .+ 3 .* sigma;\n     name = \"3 sigma\", fillcolor = \"rgba(120,120,120,0.25)\")\nxyplot!(\"Envelope\", x, prediction;\n        mode = \"lines\", name = \"Prediction\", line_color = \"cyan\")\npanel!(\"Envelope\"; xaxis_title = \"Time (s)\", yaxis_title = \"Value\")\n\nAll three arrays must have equal lengths. Draw the band first so overlaid data appear on top. band constructs a filled polygon from the bounds; it is a convenience helper rather than a separate Plotly trace type.","title":"Uncertainty bands"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Contours-and-heatmaps","page":"Data plotting","text":"For grid plots, rows of Z follow y and columns follow x: size(Z) must equal (length(y), length(x)).\n\nx = collect(range(-2.0, 2.0; length = 41))\ny = collect(range(-1.0, 1.0; length = 21))\nZ = [xi^2 + 2yi^2 for yi in y, xi in x]\n\ncontour(\"Cost contours\", x, y, Z;\n        colorscale = \"Viridis\", contours_showlabels = true,\n        colorbar_title = \"Cost\")\nheatmap(\"Cost map\", x, y, Z;\n        colorscale = \"Viridis\", colorbar_title = \"Cost\")\npanel!(\"Cost contours\"; xaxis_title = \"x\", yaxis_title = \"y\")\npanel!(\"Cost map\"; xaxis_title = \"x\", yaxis_title = \"y\")\n\nSupplying only Z uses grid indices for the coordinates. Use zmin and zmax to set color limits. The color bar explains scalar values; configure it with trace attributes such as colorbar_title, separately from the panel legend.","title":"Contours and heatmaps"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Polar-plots","page":"Data plotting","text":"scatterpolar takes angles in degrees by default. Set thetaunit = \"radians\" for angular inputs in radians. Radius values are used as supplied.\n\nazimuth_deg = collect(0.0:15.0:180.0)\nelevation_deg = 60.0 .* sin.(deg2rad.(azimuth_deg))\nscatterpolar(\"Sky view\", azimuth_deg, elevation_deg;\n             mode = \"lines+markers\", name = \"Pass\")\npanel!(\"Sky view\";\n       polar_angularaxis_direction = \"clockwise\",\n       polar_angularaxis_rotation = 90,\n       polar_radialaxis_range = [90, 0])\n\nThis convention places north at the top and zenith at the center. Convert elevation to degrees yourself: thetaunit affects only the angular coordinate. Polar layout settings begin with polar_.","title":"Polar plots"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Geographic-plots","page":"Data plotting","text":"scattergeo takes longitude in degrees east and latitude in degrees north. Use mode = \"lines\" for tracks or \"markers\" for locations:\n\nscattergeo(\"Stations\", [-116.9, -4.2, 148.98], [35.4, 40.4, -35.4];\n           mode = \"markers\", name = \"Stations\", marker_size = 8)\npanel!(\"Stations\";\n       geo_projection_type = \"equirectangular\", geo_showland = true)\n\nGeographic layout settings begin with geo_. Convert radians before plotting and use geodetic latitude when comparing positions with map coastlines. The plot does not perform coordinate-frame conversions.","title":"Geographic plots"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Three-dimensional-points,-lines,-and-surfaces","page":"Data plotting","text":"scatter3d takes three equal-length coordinate arrays. Its mode can be \"markers\", \"lines\", or \"lines+markers\". surface uses the same grid orientation as contour and heatmap.\n\nangle = collect(range(0.0, 4pi; length = 200))\nscatter3d(\"Helix\", cos.(angle), sin.(angle), angle ./ (2pi);\n          mode = \"lines\", name = \"Curve\")\npanel!(\"Helix\";\n       scene_xaxis_title = \"x\", scene_yaxis_title = \"y\",\n       scene_zaxis_title = \"Turns\", scene_aspectmode = \"data\")\n\nx = collect(range(-2.0, 2.0; length = 41))\ny = collect(range(-1.0, 1.0; length = 21))\nZ = [xi^2 + 2yi^2 for yi in y, xi in x]\nsurface(\"Cost surface\", x, y, Z;\n        colorscale = \"Viridis\", colorbar_title = \"Cost\")\npanel!(\"Cost surface\";\n       scene_xaxis_title = \"x\", scene_yaxis_title = \"y\",\n       scene_zaxis_title = \"Cost\")\n\nThree-dimensional axis settings begin with scene_. For coordinates with the same units, scene_aspectmode = \"data\" preserves relative scale. Use scatter3d! to overlay points or a curve on a surface.\n\nFor animated spacecraft trajectories around Earth, use orbitview. See 3D plotting for that interface and its playback controls.","title":"Three-dimensional points, lines, and surfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Legends-and-trace-appearance","page":"Data plotting","text":"Set legend text with trace name. For multi-component data, a scalar name such as \"Position\" produces Position[1], Position[2], and so on. Supply a vector of names to label components individually:\n\nx = collect(0.0:0.25:4.0)\nY = hcat(sin.(x), cos.(x))\nxyplot(\"Signals\", x, Y;\n       mode = \"lines\", name = [\"Sine\", \"Cosine\"],\n       line_color = [\"cyan\", \"orange\"], line_dash = [\"solid\", \"dash\"])\npanel!(\"Signals\";\n       showlegend = true, legend_title_text = \"Signal\",\n       legend_orientation = \"h\", legend_x = 0, legend_y = 1.15)\n\nPanel showlegend = true explicitly displays a legend, including for a single trace. Set showlegend = false on a trace to omit its entry, or on the panel to hide the whole legend. Give related traces the same legendgroup string to group their legend interactions.\n\nWhen a paired-series call expands into multiple traces:\n\nA scalar style value applies to every trace.\nA vector supplies one value per trace for attributes that do not accept per-point arrays, such as line_color, line_width, and line_dash.\nAttributes that accept per-point arrays, such as marker_size and marker_color, retain that meaning. Nest arrays to supply separate values to separate traces: marker_size = [sizes_a, sizes_b].\n\nFor example, marker_size = [[3], [6]] supplies a different marker-size array to each of two traces. The distribution rule does not apply to the single-trace grid, spatial, and band interfaces, or to directly constructed PlotlyBase traces.","title":"Legends and trace appearance"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Axis-labels,-ranges,-and-scales","page":"Data plotting","text":"Use panel! for layout settings and include units in labels:\n\npanel!(\"Altitude\";\n       xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\",\n       yaxis_title = \"Altitude (km)\",\n       xaxis_range = [0, 6], yaxis_range = [350, 500],\n       xaxis_showgrid = true, yaxis_showgrid = true)\n\nRanges are ordered pairs; [6, 0] reverses an axis. Set yaxis_autorange = true to restore automatic vertical limits. For a logarithmic Cartesian axis, use yaxis_type = \"log\". Explicit range endpoints then use base-10 exponents: [0, 3] displays values from 1 to 1,000. Only positive values can appear on a log axis.\n\nSet xaxis_tickvals and xaxis_ticktext to control tick positions and displayed labels. Choose layout settings for the coordinate system you are using:\n\nCoordinates Layout prefixes\nCartesian xaxis_, yaxis_\nPolar polar_angularaxis_, polar_radialaxis_\nGeographic geo_\nThree-dimensional data scene_xaxis_, scene_yaxis_, scene_zaxis_\n\nUse underscore keywords for nested properties, such as legend_orientation = \"h\", rather than nested string-keyed dictionaries.","title":"Axis labels, ranges, and scales"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Combine-plots-and-update-results","page":"Data plotting","text":"Traces with compatible coordinates can share a panel, such as a band and a Cartesian line, or a surface and 3D scatter. Keep Cartesian, polar, geographic, and three-dimensional plots in separate panels. Use separate panels for orbit views as well.\n\nopen_dashboard(\"Altitude\", \"Signals\")\nclear!(\"Signals\")   # Empty the panel, retaining its layout.\n\nclear_all!() removes all panels and their layouts from the dashboard.\n\nPlotting calls can be made during propagation or solver callbacks. Dashboard publishing is throttled and runs on a separate task. Adding calls create more traces; use the replacing form with updated arrays to refresh a complete history. Avoid creating a new trace for every sample in a long run.","title":"Combine plots and update results"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Use-PlotlyBase-traces-directly","page":"Data plotting","text":"For plot types without convenience functions, construct PlotlyBase traces and pass them to xyplot or xyplot!. EpicycleIO exports PlotlyBase, so it is available after using EpicycleIO:\n\nusing EpicycleIO\n\nsamples = [398.0, 401.0, 399.5, 402.0, 400.2]\nxyplot(\"Insertion altitude\",\n       PlotlyBase.box(y = samples, name = \"Monte Carlo\", boxmean = \"sd\"))\npanel!(\"Insertion altitude\"; yaxis_title = \"Altitude (km)\", showlegend = true)\n\nxyplot(\"Residuals\",\n       PlotlyBase.violin(y = [-0.2, 0.1, 0.05, -0.1, 0.3],\n                         name = \"Range\", box_visible = true))\n\nSupply several trace objects in one call, or combine directly constructed traces with convenience-function traces in compatible panels. Array expansion and attribute distribution do not apply to trace objects; configure each trace when constructing it.\n\nYou can also supply a layout object:\n\npanel!(\"Insertion altitude\",\n       PlotlyBase.Layout(yaxis_title = \"Altitude (km)\", showlegend = true))\n\nAdditional styles are available through trace constructors supported by the installed PlotlyBase and dashboard renderer. The panel interface distinguishes Cartesian, polar, geographic, and three-dimensional families; specialized Plotly subplot types may require configuration beyond the convenience interface.","title":"Use PlotlyBase traces directly"},{"category":"section","location":"data_plotting/#Troubleshooting","page":"Data plotting","text":"Symptom What to check\nLabels or legend settings do not change Supply layout settings to panel! using the plot's panel name.\nA single trace has no legend Provide a trace name and set panel showlegend = true.\nA call removes earlier data Use the adding form with ! to keep existing traces.\nA grid is rejected or transposed Rows must follow y and columns must follow x; check size(Z).\nPolar or geographic data look incorrect Check degrees versus radians and the coordinate-system layout settings.\nTraces cannot share a panel Use separate panels for different coordinate families.\nAn attribute-name warning appears Check spelling and whether the property belongs to a trace or the layout. The bundled schema can lag newer renderer attributes, so a warning does not always mean a property is unsupported.\n\nSee the API reference for signatures and the repository's examples directory for complete plotting scripts.","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"Every name EpicycleIO exports. For what an attribute does, see Plotly's reference — attribute names here are Plotly's own.\n\nTo see this list from the REPL rather than reading it here:\n\nnames(EpicycleIO)","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Qualified-names","page":"API","text":"These are not exported; call them with the package name, as in EpicycleIO.close_dashboard().\n\nPages = [\"api.md\"]","title":"Qualified names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.band","page":"API","text":"band([panel], x, lower, upper; attributes...)\n\nDraw a filled region between two bounds — a covariance envelope around residuals.\n\nDraw the band before the data that sits inside it, so the points land on top rather than under.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nx: the horizontal axis, shared by both bounds.\nlower, upper: the two edges, each the same length as x.\n\nNotes\n\nThis is the one wrapper with no Plotly trace behind it. Plotly has no band, so this builds a closed polygon — x forward along the upper bound and back along the lower — and fills it.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nband(\"Residuals\", t, -3 .* σ, 3 .* σ; name = \"3σ\", fillcolor = \"rgba(120,120,120,0.25)\")\nxyplot!(\"Residuals\", t, residual; mode = \"markers\", name = \"residual\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.band"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.band!","page":"API","text":"band!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as band.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.band!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.bar!","page":"API","text":"bar!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as bar.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.bar!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.clear!","page":"API","text":"clear!(panel = \"Plot\")\n\nEmpty a panel but leave it on the dashboard.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: the panel to empty. Without one you get the default panel.\n\nNotes\n\nThe layout survives: a panel set to a log axis is still a log axis after clearing, so refilling it does not mean configuring it again.\n\nClearing a panel that does not exist does nothing, silently. Emptying a script's panels before filling them, so a re-run starts fresh rather than accumulating, is an ordinary thing to write — and the first time it runs, those panels are not there yet.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The emptied panel is republished, so the browser follows.\n\nExamples\n\nclear!(\"Altitude\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.clear!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.clear_all!-Tuple{}","page":"API","text":"clear_all!()\n\nRemove every panel from the dashboard.\n\nclear! empties one panel and leaves it in place; this takes them all away, layout and all, and the next plot starts from an empty page.\n\nNotes\n\nThe browser follows. Forgetting the panels here without saying so would leave the page showing panels that no longer exist, and the next plot would appear among the ghosts.\n\nPanel files left in the served directory are deleted too, all of them rather than only this session's. The directory outlives the process, so otherwise it fills with orphans from every earlier run.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing.\n\nExamples\n\nclear_all!()          # a script that starts from a clean dashboard\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.clear_all!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.contour!","page":"API","text":"contour!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as contour.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.contour!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.coverage-Tuple{Union{SpacecraftHistory, Spacecraft}}","page":"API","text":"coverage(eph) -> Vector{Tuple{Time, Time}}\n\nThe intervals a history covers, in time order: segments that touch or overlap are merged, so a gap between segments shows as a gap between intervals. A segment's usable span, where its file gives one, limits what it covers.\n\nArguments\n\neph: A SpacecraftHistory, or a Spacecraft.\n\nReturns\n\nA vector of (start, stop) tuples.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Epicycle\nt0 = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0, 0, 0, 7.5, 1.0]), time = t0)\nprop = OrbitPropagator(ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())), IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12))\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 600.0))\nlength(coverage(sat))\n\n# output\n\n1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.coverage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.diagnostics-Tuple{SpacecraftHistory}","page":"API","text":"diagnostics(eph) -> Vector{Diagnostic}\n\nWhat the reader corrected or assumed when it read the file a history came from, as Diagnostics. Empty for a history that was not read from a file, or for a file read without corrections.\n\nArguments\n\neph: A SpacecraftHistory from read_ephemeris.\n\nReturns\n\nA vector of Diagnostic, each with its line, the field, and what was done.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Epicycle\nt0 = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0, 0, 0, 7.5, 1.0]), time = t0)\nprop = OrbitPropagator(ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())), IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12))\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 600.0))\npath = tempname() * \".bsp\"\nwrite_ephemeris(path, sat; step = 60.0, naif_id = -900001)\nfirst(diagnostics(read_ephemeris(path))).message\n\n# output\n\n\"SPICE's J2000 frame read as ICRF, as NAIF defines it\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.epochs-Tuple{Union{SpacecraftHistory, Spacecraft}}","page":"API","text":"epochs(eph) -> Vector{Time}\n\nEvery epoch in a history or a spacecraft's history, in segment order. An epoch shared by two segments, such as a maneuver time, appears once for each.\n\nArguments\n\neph: A SpacecraftHistory, or a Spacecraft.\n\nReturns\n\nA vector of Time.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Epicycle\nt0 = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0, 0, 0, 7.5, 1.0]), time = t0)\nprop = OrbitPropagator(ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())), IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12))\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 600.0))\npath = tempname() * \".oem\"\nwrite_ephemeris(path, sat; step = 60.0)\nlength(epochs(read_ephemeris(path)))\n\n# output\n\n11\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.epochs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.heatmap!","page":"API","text":"heatmap!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as heatmap.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.heatmap!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.histogram!","page":"API","text":"histogram!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as histogram.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.histogram!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.open_dashboard-Tuple{Vararg{AbstractString}}","page":"API","text":"open_dashboard()\nopen_dashboard(panel, more...)\n\nOpen the dashboard in a browser tab.\n\nWith no arguments you get every panel. Naming panels opens a tab showing only those, which is how you spread twelve plots across a few windows without splitting where they live:\n\nopen_dashboard(\"Range residuals\", \"Range-rate residuals\")   # navigation, in its own tab\nopen_dashboard(\"Porkchop\")                                  # one plot, full size\n\nThe panels are still one set on one server. A scoped tab is a view of it, so a panel that several tabs show updates in all of them.\n\nArguments\n\ntitles: the panels to show. With none, the tab shows every panel.\n\nNotes\n\nOpens a tab through the operating system's usual handler. If that fails — no browser, or a headless machine — you get a warning carrying the URL rather than an error, because failing to open a window is not a reason to stop a run.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.open_dashboard"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.orbitview!-Tuple","page":"API","text":"orbitview!([panel], sat; kwargs...)\n\nAdd a spacecraft to a view that already exists, keeping what is there.\n\n🔴 Not implemented. Several spacecraft in one view needs the CZML writer to merge entity sets, which it does not yet do.\n\nReturns\n\nNever returns. Throws an ArgumentError naming the way to get the same picture today — give the second spacecraft its own panel with orbitview. It refuses rather than quietly drawing one spacecraft and dropping the other.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.orbitview!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.orbitview-Tuple","page":"API","text":"orbitview([panel], sat; kwargs...)\n\nDraw a spacecraft's propagated trajectory in three dimensions, with a clock you can play.\n\nThe panel joins the dashboard beside any plots, so a trajectory and the quantities describing it share one page.\n\nEverything is read from the spacecraft:\n\nthe trajectory, from sat.history\nthe epoch, from the earliest recorded sample\none colored arc per propagation segment, from the same palette the plots use\nthe name shown on the entity, from sat.name\n\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationDays(), 1.0))\n\norbitview(sat)\norbitview(\"Mission\", sat)          # name the panel\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nsat: the spacecraft. It must have been propagated — the trajectory is read from its history.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\ncolors: palette for the arcs, cycled. Defaults to the shared palette, so a trajectory and the plots of it match.\nlinewidth: arc width in pixels.\nspeed: initial playback rate, in simulated seconds per real second. :auto fits the whole trajectory into about two minutes.\n\nNotes\n\nPositions go to the browser in the inertial frame and Cesium applies the rotation to fixed itself, so the orbit holds still against the stars and the body turns beneath it. The epoch is converted to UTC on the way out, whatever scale the history was recorded in, because Cesium reads times as UTC.\n\nAn impulsive burn is marked with a point labeled with its ΔV, differenced from the velocities either side of it rather than taken from whatever commanded it.\n\nEarth is the only central body today.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The view is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError if the spacecraft has no propagated history, or if nothing in that history is long enough to draw an arc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.orbitview"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.panel!","page":"API","text":"panel!([panel]; layout...)\npanel!([panel], layout::PlotlyBase.Layout)\n\nSet a panel's titles, axis labels, scales and limits.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: the panel to configure. Without one you get the default panel.\nlayout...: Plotly layout attributes. Nested settings are spelled with underscores, so yaxis_type reaches Plotly as yaxis.type.\n\nNotes\n\nSettings accumulate rather than replace, so calling this twice for the same axis keeps both. Limits are an ordered pair, so an axis runs backwards if you give them backwards. A Dict is not a way in — write legend_orientation = \"h\", not legend = Dict(\"orientation\" => \"h\").\n\nConfiguring a panel that does not exist yet is allowed and creates it empty, which is what lets you set a panel up before plotting into it. A name that was meant to reach an existing panel and does not earns a warning naming the panels there are.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is republished with the new layout.\n\nExamples\n\npanel!(\"Altitude\"; xaxis_title = \"hours from epoch\", yaxis_title = \"km\", yaxis_type = \"log\")\n\npanel!(\"Decay\"; xaxis_range = [6, 0])            # runs backwards\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.panel!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.position_difference-Tuple{Union{SpacecraftHistory, Spacecraft}, Union{SpacecraftHistory, Spacecraft}}","page":"API","text":"position_difference(a, b) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe distance between two trajectories at each epoch of a that b covers, in km.\n\nArguments\n\na: The reference, a SpacecraftHistory or a Spacecraft.\nb: The trajectory compared with it, a SpacecraftHistory or a Spacecraft.\n\nNotes\n\nb is interpolated at a's epochs and expressed in a's coordinate system before the difference is taken. Epochs of a that b does not cover are skipped.\nAt an epoch two segments of b share, the later segment applies, as in state_at.\n\nReturns\n\nA vector of distances in km, one per epoch of a covered by b.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Epicycle\nt0 = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0, 0, 0, 7.5, 1.0]), time = t0)\nprop = OrbitPropagator(ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())), IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12))\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 1800.0))\npath = tempname() * \".oem\"\nwrite_ephemeris(path, sat; step = 60.0)\nmaximum(position_difference(read_ephemeris(path), sat)) < 1e-6\n\n# output\n\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.position_difference"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.read_ephemeris-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_ephemeris(path; format, kwargs...) -> SpacecraftHistory\n\nRead an ephemeris file into a SpacecraftHistory: CCSDS OEM, STK .e or JPL SPK.\n\nArguments\n\npath: The file. Its extension, .oem, .e or .bsp, chooses the format.\nformat: OEM(), STKEphemeris() or SPK(), when the extension does not say.\nstrict: Treat every correction as an error. Default false.\nskip_bad_lines: OEM and STK. Drop an unreadable data line, with a warning, and split the segment there. Default false.\nref_frame, center: Names to read in place of the file's frame and center, such as \"EME2000\" and \"EARTH\". time_system: OEM only. Each override is recorded in the diagnostics with the file's original value.\nnaif_id: SPK. The object to read, when the file holds more than one.\nstep: SPK. Seconds between samples for SPK types that store none, such as Chebyshev.\n\nNotes\n\nEach segment of the file is a segment of the history, in the coordinate system the file names. Interpolation never crosses from one segment to the next.\nA file's problems are reported all at once. What can be corrected without changing the data is corrected and listed in one warning; the rest stops the read with an EphemerisReadError giving every problem with its line. diagnostics returns what was corrected or assumed.\nBeyond times and states, each segment's metadata holds the declared interpolation, accelerations and covariance where the file has them (km, km/s, km/s², km² and so on), the object's name and ID, the usable span, and the file's details.\n\nReturns\n\nA SpacecraftHistory, one segment per file segment.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Epicycle\nt0 = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0, 0, 0, 7.5, 1.0]), time = t0, name = \"SAT\")\nprop = OrbitPropagator(ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())), IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12))\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 1800.0))\npath = tempname() * \".oem\"\nwrite_ephemeris(path, sat; step = 60.0)\neph = read_ephemeris(path)\nlength(epochs(eph))\n\n# output\n\n31\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.read_ephemeris"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.report-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"report(path; columns...)\n\nWrite named columns to a delimited text file.\n\nThe keywords become the column headings, so they are your words rather than whatever the quantity happens to be called — someone who did not write the run has to read this. A vector-valued column expands into components, so position becomes position_1, position_2, position_3.\n\nreport(\"flight.txt\"; time = [x.jd for x in t], position = r, velocity = v)\n\nArguments\n\npath: the file to write. An existing file is overwritten.\ndelim: what separates the columns. Two spaces by default.\ncolumns...: the data, one keyword per column. Every column must be the same length.\n\nNotes\n\nColumns carry no units of their own — they are written as given, so name the keyword for what the number is (altitude_km) if the reader will need to know.\n\nNumbers are written exactly: integers as integers, and floats in the shortest form that reads back to the same value, so a Julian date keeps its full precision.\n\nValues must render on one line. An epoch does not, so convert it first — t.isot, t.mjd or t.jd — and the file then records which scale and format you meant. A text value may not contain the delimiter, or any whitespace with the default delimiter, since that would split its column.\n\nReturns\n\nThe path written, so it can be passed straight on to something that reads it.\n\nThrows an ArgumentError if no columns are given, if the columns differ in length, or if a value's printed form spans lines or contains the delimiter and would break a row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.report"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.scatter3d!","page":"API","text":"scatter3d!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as scatter3d.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.scatter3d!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.scattergeo!","page":"API","text":"scattergeo!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as scattergeo.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.scattergeo!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.scatterpolar!","page":"API","text":"scatterpolar!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as scatterpolar.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.scatterpolar!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.span-Tuple{Union{SpacecraftHistory, Spacecraft}}","page":"API","text":"span(eph) -> Tuple{Time, Time}\n\nThe start and end of a history's coverage: its first and last epochs, or its usable span where a segment declares one. A history may have gaps inside its span; see coverage.\n\nArguments\n\neph: A SpacecraftHistory, or a Spacecraft.\n\nReturns\n\nA tuple (first, last).\n\nExamples\n\nusing Epicycle\nt0 = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0, 0, 0, 7.5, 1.0]), time = t0)\nprop = OrbitPropagator(ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())), IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12))\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 600.0))\nt_start, t_stop = span(sat)\nround((t_stop - t_start) * 86400; digits = 6)\n\n# output\n\n600.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.span"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.state_at-Tuple{Union{SpacecraftHistory, Spacecraft}, Time}","page":"API","text":"state_at(eph, t; overlap = :error) -> Coordinate\n\nThe interpolated state at t, with its coordinate system and epoch.\n\nArguments\n\neph: A SpacecraftHistory, or a Spacecraft.\nt: The epoch, in any time scale.\noverlap: Where two segments' usable spans overlap: :error (default), :earlier or :later.\n\nNotes\n\nThe segment that covers t is interpolated with its declared method, Lagrange degree 7 when none is declared, and never with points from another segment.\nAt an epoch two segments share, such as a maneuver, the later segment applies: the state after the event.\nt outside every segment, inside a gap, is an ArgumentError naming the nearest covered times. A time within 1 µs of a segment's first or last epoch is taken as that epoch, since files store epochs to finite precision.\nHermite interpolation uses the tabulated velocities. Lagrange interpolates positions and velocities independently, as OEM and STK readers do; where a file has positions only, the velocity is the derivative of the position polynomial.\n\nReturns\n\nA Coordinate in the segment's coordinate system.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Epicycle\nt0 = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0, 0, 0, 7.5, 1.0]), time = t0)\nprop = OrbitPropagator(ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())), IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12))\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 600.0))\nc = state_at(sat, Time(\"2026-10-21T00:05:00\", UTC(), ISOT()))\nround(to_vector(CartesianState(c))[1]; digits = 3)\n\n# output\n\n6637.168\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.state_at"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.surface!","page":"API","text":"surface!([panel], data...; attributes...)\n\nAdd to a panel instead of replacing what is in it. Takes the same arguments and attributes as surface.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.surface!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.write_ephemeris-Tuple{AbstractString, Union{SpacecraftHistory, Spacecraft}}","page":"API","text":"write_ephemeris(path, source; format, coordinate_system, step, interpolation, time_system,\n                naif_id, object_name, object_id, originator, version, message_id,\n                classification, digits,\n                discard_unsupported) -> String\n\nWrite a spacecraft's trajectory, or any SpacecraftHistory, to an ephemeris file: CCSDS OEM, STK .e or JPL SPK.\n\nArguments\n\npath: The file to write. Its extension, .oem, .e or .bsp, chooses the format; it is replaced if it exists.\nsource: A Spacecraft, whose history is written, or a SpacecraftHistory, such as one from read_ephemeris.\nformat: OEM(), STKEphemeris() or SPK(), when the extension does not say.\ncoordinate_system: Write in this coordinate system, converting every state. Default: each segment's own, except for SPK, which is written in ICRF axes about the segment's origin.\nstep: Resample every segment every step seconds, each segment's first and last epochs included, by interpolating it. Default nothing: the epochs as they are.\ninterpolation: The method the file declares, such as Hermite(7). Default: what the source declares, else Lagrange(7).\ntime_system: OEM. The time system epochs are written in, e.g. UTC() (default) or TDB(), or \"GPS\".\nnaif_id: SPK. The object's NAIF ID; required for SPK.\nobject_name, object_id: The object. Default: the source's, else the spacecraft's name, else \"UNKNOWN\".\noriginator: OEM. Who made the file. Default \"EPICYCLE\".\nversion: OEM. \"3.0\" (default) or \"2.0\".\nmessage_id: OEM 3.0. The new message's ID. Default none: a file written is a new message, so the source's ID is not reused.\nclassification: OEM 3.0. The file's marking. Default: the source's, for a history read from a marked OEM. STK and SPK cannot carry a marking, so a marked source is refused unless classification = \"\" is given.\ndigits: OEM and STK. Decimal places of seconds in written epochs. Default 9. An OEM is refused if rounding would move an epoch by more than 1 ns, which would shift the trajectory.\ndiscard_unsupported: Write without what the format cannot hold, rather than refuse: accelerations and covariance in SPK, a usable span narrower than the data in STK. Default false.\n\nNotes\n\nEvery rule of the format is checked before the file is opened, and every problem is reported in one ArgumentError. Nothing is written on failure, and an existing file is left as it was.\nEach segment of the history is a segment of the file, so a maneuver stays a discontinuity. The one-state segment maneuver! records repeats the first state of the propagation after it and is not written.\nWhat the source carries beyond states (accelerations, covariance) is written where the format can hold it. A conversion to another coordinate system rotates states only: a source with accelerations or covariance in another frame is refused rather than written half-converted.\n\nReturns\n\npath.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Epicycle\nt0 = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT())\nsat = Spacecraft(state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0, 0, 0, 7.5, 1.0]), time = t0)\nprop = OrbitPropagator(ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())), IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12))\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 600.0))\npath = tempname() * \".e\"\nwrite_ephemeris(path, sat; step = 60.0, interpolation = Hermite(7))\nfilter(startswith(\"InterpolationSamplesM1\"), readlines(path))\n\n# output\n\n1-element Vector{String}:\n \"InterpolationSamplesM1  3\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.write_ephemeris"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.xyplot!-Tuple","page":"API","text":"xyplot!([panel], x, y; attributes...)\n\nAdd a line to a panel instead of replacing what is there. Takes everything xyplot does.\n\nLines need not be the same length, so two spacecraft sampled differently share a panel without any fuss.\n\nSafe to call from inside a propagation or a solver callback: writes are throttled and happen on their own task, so publishing cannot slow the run feeding it. That task runs when Julia yields, at any sleep, I/O or yield(), so a tight loop that does none of these should call yield() after plotting for the dashboard to update during the loop. A zoom you set survives the update, where xyplot resets the view because the data underneath it changed.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is redrawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nxyplot(\"Altitude\",  t_a, alt_a; name = \"Sat A\")\nxyplot!(\"Altitude\", t_b, alt_b; name = \"Sat B\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.xyplot!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.xyplot-Tuple","page":"API","text":"xyplot([panel], x, y; attributes...)\nxyplot([panel], y; attributes...)\n\nDraw a line, replacing whatever was in the panel.\n\nThe leading string names a panel; without one you get the default panel. Plotting into a name that already exists replaces it, so re-running a script gives you one figure rather than twenty. Use xyplot! to add instead.\n\nAttributes are Plotly's, passed through untouched:\n\nxyplot(t, altitude; name = \"altitude\", mode = \"lines\", line_color = \"cyan\", line_width = 2)\n\nA matrix or a vector of vectors draws one line per component, which is what a position column from history already is:\n\nxyplot(t, r; name = [\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"], line_color = [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"])\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nx, y: paired arrays. Several pairs may follow one another, each with its own length. Given y alone, the horizontal axis is its index.\n\nNotes\n\nx and y are plain arrays and carry no units, frame or time system of their own — whatever you computed them in is what gets drawn, so label the axes with panel!. A Time is not a number: render it first, or subtract the epoch to get hours.\n\nA NaN or an Inf draws as a break in the line rather than raising.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard, and a browser tab opens if none is watching.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.xyplot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotlyBase.bar","page":"API","text":"bar([panel], categories, values; attributes...)\n\nDraw one bar per category — ΔV by maneuver, passes by station.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\ncategories: the label for each bar. Strings or numbers.\nvalues: bar heights, one per category.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nbar(\"ΔV budget\", [\"TOI\", \"MCC\", \"MOI\"], [2.82, 1.14, 0.48])\npanel!(\"ΔV budget\"; yaxis_title = \"km/s\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotlyBase.bar"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotlyBase.contour","page":"API","text":"contour([panel], x, y, Z; attributes...)\ncontour([panel], Z; attributes...)\n\nDraw a scalar field over a grid, as labeled contour lines — a porkchop plot, a coverage map.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nx: the horizontal axis, one value per column of Z.\ny: the vertical axis, one value per row of Z.\nZ: the field, indexed [row, column], so size(Z) == (length(y), length(x)).\n\nGiven Z alone, the axes are its indices.\n\nNotes\n\nRows follow y and columns follow x. That is Plotly's convention, and it is why the horizontal axis is the second index rather than the first — the shape is checked, so a transposed grid is refused rather than drawn sideways.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\ncontour(\"Porkchop\", departure_days, arrival_days, C3;\n         colorscale = \"Viridis\", colorbar_title = \"C3 km²/s²\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotlyBase.contour"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotlyBase.heatmap","page":"API","text":"heatmap([panel], x, y, Z; attributes...)\nheatmap([panel], Z; attributes...)\n\nDraw a scalar field over a grid, as colored cells — a porkchop plot, a coverage map.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nx: the horizontal axis, one value per column of Z.\ny: the vertical axis, one value per row of Z.\nZ: the field, indexed [row, column], so size(Z) == (length(y), length(x)).\n\nGiven Z alone, the axes are its indices.\n\nNotes\n\nRows follow y and columns follow x. That is Plotly's convention, and it is why the horizontal axis is the second index rather than the first — the shape is checked, so a transposed grid is refused rather than drawn sideways.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nheatmap(\"Porkchop\", departure_days, arrival_days, C3;\n         colorscale = \"Viridis\", colorbar_title = \"C3 km²/s²\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotlyBase.heatmap"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotlyBase.histogram","page":"API","text":"histogram([panel], values; attributes...)\n\nDraw how a set of values is distributed — residuals, Monte Carlo misses.\n\nPlotly chooses the bins. Set nbinsx for an upper bound on their number, or xbins_size to fix the width.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nvalues: the sample. One array, not a pair.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nhistogram(\"Range residuals\", residuals; nbinsx = 40)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotlyBase.histogram"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotlyBase.scatter3d","page":"API","text":"scatter3d([panel], x, y, z; attributes...)\n\nDraw a line or points in three axes of data — a B-plane, a parameter sweep.\n\nThis is a data plot with three axes, not a view of a body. For a trajectory around a central body, with a clock and a globe, use orbitview.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nx, y, z: one value each per point, all the same length.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nscatter3d(\"B-plane\", b_dot_r, b_dot_t, epoch_offset; mode = \"markers\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotlyBase.scatter3d"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotlyBase.scattergeo","page":"API","text":"scattergeo([panel], longitude, latitude; attributes...)\n\nDraw a track on a map of the body — a ground track, a station network.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nlongitude: degrees east, in the body-fixed frame you computed them in.\nlatitude: degrees north, same frame.\n\nNotes\n\nDegrees, not radians, and geodetic rather than geocentric latitude if you want the track to sit on the coastlines the way a map expects. The projection and the coastlines are Plotly's, set through panel! with geo_ attributes.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nscattergeo(\"Ground track\", lon_deg, lat_deg; mode = \"lines\", name = \"Explorer\")\npanel!(\"Ground track\"; geo_projection_type = \"equirectangular\", geo_showland = true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotlyBase.scattergeo"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotlyBase.scatterpolar","page":"API","text":"scatterpolar([panel], θ, r; attributes...)\n\nDraw against angle and radius — a ground station pass, an antenna pattern.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel. Without one you get the default panel.\nθ: angle, in degrees unless you pass thetaunit = \"radians\".\nr: radius, in whatever units the radial axis is set to.\n\nNotes\n\nthetaunit converts the angle for you. There is no equivalent for the radial axis, so an elevation in radians plotted against a degree range puts every point on the rim — convert it yourself. A polar trace cannot share a panel with a cartesian one; Plotly draws them on different subplot types.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nscatterpolar(\"Sky view\", azimuth, rad2deg.(elevation); mode = \"lines\", name = \"Pass 3\")\n\npanel!(\"Sky view\";\n       polar_angularaxis_direction = \"clockwise\",   # compass sense\n       polar_angularaxis_rotation  = 90,            # north at the top\n       polar_radialaxis_range      = [90, 0])       # zenith at the center\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotlyBase.scatterpolar"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PlotlyBase.surface","page":"API","text":"surface([panel], x, y, Z; attributes...)\nsurface([panel], Z; attributes...)\n\nDraw a scalar field over a grid, as a surface in three axes — a porkchop plot, a coverage map.\n\nArguments\n\npanel: optional leading string naming the panel.\nx: the horizontal axis, one value per column of Z.\ny: the vertical axis, one value per row of Z.\nZ: the field, indexed [row, column], so size(Z) == (length(y), length(x)).\n\nGiven Z alone, the axes are its indices.\n\nNotes\n\nRows follow y and columns follow x. That is Plotly's convention, and it is why the horizontal axis is the second index rather than the first — the shape is checked, so a transposed grid is refused rather than drawn sideways.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The panel is drawn on the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nsurface(\"Porkchop\", departure_days, arrival_days, C3;\n         colorscale = \"Viridis\", colorbar_title = \"C3 km²/s²\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlotlyBase.surface"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.EphemerisReadError","page":"API","text":"EphemerisReadError(path, problems)\n\nThe error thrown when an ephemeris file has problems the reader cannot correct. It lists every problem found, not only the first.\n\nFields\n\npath: The file read.\nproblems: Every Diagnostic of severity :error, in file order.\nfixed: The problems that were corrected, shown after the errors for context.\nhint: A suggestion for how to proceed, or an empty string.\n\nExamples\n\ne = EpicycleIO.EphemerisReadError(\"a.oem\",\n        [EpicycleIO.Diagnostic(:error, 14, \"REF_FRAME\", \"EME200 is not a known frame. Did you mean EME2000?\")],\n        EpicycleIO.Diagnostic[], \"\")\nlength(e.problems)\n\n# output\n\n1\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.EphemerisReadError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.OEM","page":"API","text":"OEM()\n\nThe CCSDS Orbit Ephemeris Message format, .oem. Passed as format = when the extension does not say which format a file is in.\n\nExamples\n\nOEM()\n\n# output\n\nOEM()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.OEM"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.SPK","page":"API","text":"SPK()\n\nThe JPL SPK format, .bsp. Passed as format = when the extension does not say which format a file is in.\n\nExamples\n\nSPK()\n\n# output\n\nSPK()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.SPK"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.STKEphemeris","page":"API","text":"STKEphemeris()\n\nThe STK ephemeris format, .e. Passed as format = when the extension does not say which format a file is in.\n\nExamples\n\nSTKEphemeris()\n\n# output\n\nSTKEphemeris()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.STKEphemeris"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.close_dashboard","page":"API","text":"close_dashboard()\n\nStop the local server.\n\nAn open tab stops updating and reports that it is disconnected. Your panels are untouched — they live in Julia, not in the page — so the next plot starts the server again and a new tab picks up where this one left off.\n\nNotes\n\nCalling this when nothing is serving does nothing.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing.\n\nExamples\n\nclose_dashboard()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.close_dashboard"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.dashboard_url","page":"API","text":"dashboard_url(panels...)\n\nThe dashboard's address. Naming panels scopes the page to just those, which is how one browser tab shows a subset without the panels themselves being anywhere different.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.dashboard_url"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.auto_open!","page":"API","text":"auto_open!(on::Bool)\n\nWhether drawing opens a browser tab when none is watching. On by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.auto_open!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.narrate","page":"API","text":"narrate(text = \"\"; title = \"\", subtitle = \"\", code = \"\", facts = Pair[], status = \"\",\n        panels = String[])\n\nShow a caption across the top of the dashboard.\n\nEach call replaces what the strip shows, so a presenter's script calls it once per moment. The strip appears when there is something to show and disappears after clear_narration.\n\nArguments\n\ntext: The narration, as plain text. Line breaks are kept.\ntitle: A heading, such as the name of a section of a tour.\nsubtitle: A line under the heading.\ncode: Julia source to show beside the text, highlighted. Show the code that actually ran, so what a reader copies is what produced the plots.\nfacts: Labeled values to set out as a small table, as \"label\" => \"value\" pairs. Values are shown as given, so format numbers and units before passing them.\nstatus: A short note on what is happening now, such as \"solving…\" or \"done in 1.2 s\".\npanels: The panel titles this caption describes. A tab opened with open_dashboard(panels...) for exactly those panels shows this caption and keeps it after later captions go to other panels. The full dashboard always shows the latest caption.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing. The caption is published to the dashboard.\n\nExamples\n\nnarrate(\"Propagating a quarter of a day with the Moon and Sun as third bodies.\";\n        title  = \"A spacecraft in orbit\",\n        code   = \"propagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationDays(), 0.25))\",\n        facts  = [\"inclination\" => \"51.6 deg\", \"period\" => \"92.6 min\"],\n        panels = [\"Orbit\", \"Altitude\"])\n\nclear_narration()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.narrate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.clear_narration","page":"API","text":"clear_narration()\n\nRemove the caption strip from the dashboard, including captions kept for scoped tabs.\n\nReturns\n\nnothing.\n\nExamples\n\nclear_narration()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.clear_narration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EpicycleIO.Diagnostic","page":"API","text":"Diagnostic\n\nOne problem found while reading an ephemeris file.\n\nFields\n\nseverity: :fixed for a departure from the format that the reader corrected, :error for one it could not, and :note for an assumption made reading a valid file, such as a caller's override or ITRF2000 read as Epicycle's ITRF.\nline: The line number in the file, or 0 when the problem is not on one line.\nfield: The keyword or part of the file involved, such as \"REF_FRAME\" or \"data\".\nmessage: What was found, what was expected, and what was done or needs doing.\n\nExamples\n\nd = EpicycleIO.Diagnostic(:fixed, 3, \"CREATION_DATE\", \"keyword in lower case; read as CREATION_DATE\")\nd.severity\n\n# output\n\n:fixed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EpicycleIO.Diagnostic"},{"category":"section","location":"reporting/#Reporting","page":"Reporting","text":"Use report to write named data columns to a text file. Reports are useful for reviewing simulation results, sharing selected quantities, and importing data into other analysis tools. Each keyword supplies a column name and its values; the file contains a header followed by one row per sample.","title":"Reporting"},{"category":"section","location":"reporting/#Write-a-report","page":"Reporting","text":"This example writes three samples of elapsed time and altitude:\n\nusing EpicycleIO\n\nelapsed_s = [0.0, 60.0, 120.0]\naltitude_km = [400.0, 401.5, 403.0]\n\npath = report(\"flight.txt\";\n              elapsed_s = elapsed_s,\n              altitude_km = altitude_km)\n\nThe file contains the following values, with columns padded for readability:\n\nelapsed_s                 altitude_km\n0.0                       400.0\n60.0                      401.5\n120.0                     403.0\n\nColumns appear in the order supplied. The keyword names become the headings exactly as written. Include units in those names where useful: altitude_km is more informative than altitude. The writer does not convert units or add metadata such as the coordinate system or time scale.\n\nreport returns the output path. Relative paths are resolved from Julia's current working directory. Create the destination directory before writing. An existing file is overwritten; each call writes a complete report rather than appending rows.","title":"Write a report"},{"category":"section","location":"reporting/#Include-vector-data","page":"Reporting","text":"A column whose samples are vectors expands into one output column per component. Supply a vector of sample vectors, with one sample for each row:\n\nposition_km = [[7000.0, 0.0, 0.0],\n               [6985.0, 450.0, 0.0],\n               [6940.0, 898.0, 0.0]]\n\nreport(\"position.txt\";\n       elapsed_s = elapsed_s,\n       position_km = position_km)\n\nThe headings are elapsed_s, position_km_1, position_km_2, and position_km_3. Component numbers follow the order within each sample. Use vectors with the same number of components throughout the series.\n\nTo choose individual component names, supply them as separate columns:\n\nreport(\"position.txt\";\n       elapsed_s = elapsed_s,\n       x_km = [r[1] for r in position_km],\n       y_km = [r[2] for r in position_km],\n       z_km = [r[3] for r in position_km])\n\nThe report does not record the position's reference frame. Identify it in the headings or accompanying documentation when sharing the file.","title":"Include vector data"},{"category":"section","location":"reporting/#Report-epochs","page":"Reporting","text":"Convert Epicycle Time values to numbers or single-line strings before passing them to report. For an existing sequence of epochs, sample_epochs, choose the representation needed by the reader:\n\n# sample_epochs contains Time values expressed in UTC.\nreport(\"epochs.txt\";\n       epoch_utc_isot = [t.isot for t in sample_epochs])\n\nreport(\"julian_dates.txt\";\n       epoch_utc_jd = [t.jd for t in sample_epochs])\n\nUse t.mjd for modified Julian dates. These examples assume the epochs are already in UTC; a column name does not change their time scale. Label the heading with the actual scale and representation so readers can interpret the values correctly.\n\nPassing Time objects directly raises an ArgumentError because their printed representation spans multiple lines.","title":"Report epochs"},{"category":"section","location":"reporting/#Choose-a-delimiter","page":"Reporting","text":"By default, report separates columns with two spaces and pads each field to a minimum width of 24 characters. Set delim to use another separator:\n\nreport(\"flight.tsv\";\n       delim = \"\\t\",\n       elapsed_s = elapsed_s,\n       altitude_km = altitude_km)\n\nPadding is retained when you change the delimiter. A comma delimiter is also accepted; the writer does not quote or escape fields, so a value containing the delimiter is refused, and with the default delimiter so is a value containing any whitespace. Configure the receiving tool to trim padding if needed. The filename extension does not select the delimiter.","title":"Choose a delimiter"},{"category":"section","location":"reporting/#Formatting-and-data-requirements","page":"Reporting","text":"Integers are written as integers. Other real numbers are written as floating-point values in the shortest form that reads back to the same value, so a Julian date keeps its full precision. Other values are written using their string representation.\n\nBefore writing a report:\n\nSupply at least one named column.\nGive every column the same number of samples.\nAlign the samples yourself: values at the same index become one row. The writer does not match epochs, interpolate data, or resample series.\nConvert values whose printed representation spans multiple lines to numbers or single-line strings.\n\nMissing columns, unequal column lengths, multiline values, and values containing the delimiter raise an ArgumentError. Column names and lengths are checked before the file is opened, but values are formatted while writing. A formatting error can therefore leave a partially written file or replace an existing file with incomplete output.\n\nFor trajectory files that carry epochs, coordinate systems, and other metadata in a supported exchange format, see Ephemeris files.","title":"Formatting and data requirements"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#The-dashboard","page":"Dashboard","text":"The EpicycleIO dashboard brings interactive plots and animated spacecraft trajectories into one browser page. Use it to compare results, inspect a propagation, or watch a solver converge while your Julia session runs.\n\nYour first plotting call starts a local server and opens the dashboard in your default browser. Subsequent calls update the same set of panels. No account or API key is required.","title":"The dashboard"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Create-your-first-dashboard","page":"Dashboard","text":"Create two panels to compare altitude and speed:\n\nusing EpicycleIO\n\nhours = collect(0.0:0.1:6.0)\naltitude_km = 400.0 .+ 20.0 .* sin.(hours)\nspeed_km_s = 7.67 .- 0.01 .* sin.(hours)\n\nxyplot(\"Altitude\", hours, altitude_km; mode = \"lines\", name = \"Nominal\")\npanel!(\"Altitude\";\n       xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\", yaxis_title = \"Altitude (km)\")\n\nxyplot(\"Speed\", hours, speed_km_s; mode = \"lines\", name = \"Nominal\")\npanel!(\"Speed\";\n       xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\", yaxis_title = \"Speed (km/s)\")\n\nBoth panels appear in the same dashboard. Hover over a plot to inspect values and use the plot's toolbar to explore the data. Animated trajectory panels created with orbitview can appear alongside data plots; see 3D plotting for trajectory setup and playback controls.","title":"Create your first dashboard"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Arrange-panels-in-the-browser","page":"Dashboard","text":"Use the grid / tabs button at the top of the dashboard to switch layouts:\n\nGrid shows panels together for comparison. The number of columns adjusts to the number of panels.\nTabs shows one panel at a time. Select a panel's name to display it.\n\nTo give a group of panels its own browser tab, pass their names to open_dashboard:\n\nopen_dashboard(\"Altitude\", \"Speed\")  # Show these two panels.\nopen_dashboard(\"Altitude\")           # Give one panel the full view.\nopen_dashboard()                     # Show all panels.\n\nEach browser tab is a view of the same panels in Julia. Updating a panel updates every tab that displays it. A tab opened with selected panel names continues to show only those panels as you create others.","title":"Arrange panels in the browser"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Add-data-and-reuse-panels","page":"Dashboard","text":"The leading string in a plotting call identifies the destination panel. Omit it to use the default panel, \"Plot\". The name keyword labels a data series, also called a trace, in the panel's legend.\n\nThe replacing and adding forms let you control what happens to existing data:\n\nCall Effect\nxyplot(\"Altitude\", x, y) Replace the panel's data and reset its interactive view.\nxyplot!(\"Altitude\", x, y) Add a trace while keeping existing traces and the current zoom.\npanel!(\"Altitude\"; ...) Update layout settings while keeping previously supplied settings.\n\nFor example, add a second altitude history to the first example:\n\nxyplot!(\"Altitude\", hours, altitude_km .+ 30;\n        mode = \"lines\", name = \"Alternative\", line_dash = \"dash\")\npanel!(\"Altitude\"; showlegend = true)\n\nThe panel now contains two traces, Nominal and Alternative. Reusing panel names lets you rerun a script without creating duplicate panels. When rerunning a sequence of adding calls, first replace or clear the panel to avoid accumulating copies of the traces.\n\nThe other data plotting functions use the same replacing and adding convention. See Data plotting for plot types and accepted data shapes.","title":"Add data and reuse panels"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Configure-titles-and-axes","page":"Dashboard","text":"Use panel! for titles, axis labels, limits, and legends. Keyword names follow Plotly layout properties, with underscores for nested settings:\n\npanel!(\"Altitude\";\n       title = \"Altitude comparison\",\n       xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\",\n       yaxis_title = \"Altitude (km)\",\n       xaxis_range = [0, 6],\n       yaxis_range = [350, 500],\n       legend_orientation = \"h\")\n\nLayout settings survive data replacement. A later panel! call changes only the properties you supply. For example, xaxis_range = [6, 0] reverses the horizontal axis, and yaxis_autorange = true restores automatic vertical limits.\n\nYou can configure a panel before adding data. If other panels already exist, panel! warns when the supplied name is new. Check the spelling if you intended to update an existing panel.","title":"Configure titles and axes"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Update-results-during-a-run","page":"Dashboard","text":"Call plotting functions from a propagation or solver callback to display results as they become available. Publishing is asynchronous, and rapid updates are combined; the browser may skip intermediate states.\n\nUpdates reach the browser only when Julia has a chance to run other tasks, which happens at any sleep, I/O, or yield(). A tight loop that does none of these holds back every update until it finishes; call yield() after plotting in such a loop.\n\nFor a growing history, keep the accumulated values and replace the plotted series at a suitable interval:\n\niterations = Int[]\ncosts = Float64[]\n\nfor k in 1:100\n    push!(iterations, k)\n    push!(costs, 10.0 / k)  # Replace with the current solver cost.\n    if k % 10 == 0\n        xyplot(\"Convergence\", iterations, costs; mode = \"lines\", name = \"Cost\")\n    end\nend\npanel!(\"Convergence\"; xaxis_title = \"Iteration\", yaxis_title = \"Cost\")\n\nEach xyplot! call creates a new trace; it does not append samples to an existing trace. Use it to compare separate series rather than to add every sample of a long run. Replacing the data resets the view to fit the new results.","title":"Update results during a run"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Add-captions-to-a-demonstration","page":"Dashboard","text":"Use EpicycleIO.narrate to explain results above the panels during a tutorial, presentation, or live run. A caption can include a heading, explanatory text, labeled values, a status message, and a code sample:\n\nEpicycleIO.narrate(\"Compare the nominal and alternative altitude histories.\";\n    title = \"Altitude comparison\",\n    subtitle = \"Six-hour example\",\n    facts = [\"Duration\" => \"6 h\", \"Altitude offset\" => \"30 km\"],\n    status = \"Ready to inspect\",\n    code = \"open_dashboard(\\\"Altitude\\\", \\\"Speed\\\")\",\n    panels = [\"Altitude\", \"Speed\"])\n\nopen_dashboard(\"Altitude\", \"Speed\")\n\nEach call replaces the current caption. Text is plain text, with line breaks preserved. Format numbers and units before passing them in facts. The code field displays source text; it does not execute it.\n\nThe full dashboard shows the latest caption. Supplying panels also keeps a caption for browser tabs showing exactly that set of panels, in any order. Those tabs retain their caption when later captions describe other panel sets.\n\nRemove all captions with:\n\nEpicycleIO.clear_narration()","title":"Add captions to a demonstration"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Clear-results-and-manage-the-session","page":"Dashboard","text":"Function Effect\nclear!(\"Altitude\") Empty the panel, retaining its layout and place on the dashboard.\nclear!() Empty the default panel, \"Plot\".\nclear_all!() Remove every panel and its layout.\nEpicycleIO.close_dashboard() Stop the local server, retaining the panels in Julia.\n\nClearing a panel that does not exist has no effect. After stopping the server, the next plotting call starts it again.\n\nClosing a browser tab leaves the panels in Julia. If no dashboard viewer is detected, a later plotting call can open another tab. Panels belong to the current Julia session; ending that session ends live updates.","title":"Clear results and manage the session"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Control-automatic-browser-opening","page":"Dashboard","text":"For an automated run, disable automatic browser opening before plotting:\n\nEpicycleIO.auto_open!(false)\n\nPlotting and publishing remain enabled. Open the dashboard explicitly with open_dashboard(), or restore automatic opening with EpicycleIO.auto_open!(true).\n\nTo obtain the address without opening a browser, use:\n\nEpicycleIO.dashboard_url()\nEpicycleIO.dashboard_url(\"Altitude\", \"Speed\")\n\nThese calls start the local server if needed. The address uses 127.0.0.1 and is intended for a browser on the same machine as Julia.","title":"Control automatic browser opening"},{"category":"section","location":"dashboard/#Browser-requirements-and-troubleshooting","page":"Dashboard","text":"The dashboard loads Plotly.js from a public content delivery network. It loads CesiumJS when a trajectory view is first displayed, and trajectory views request Earth imagery from an external map service. An internet connection is needed when these resources are not cached by the browser.\n\nSymptom What to check\nThe browser does not open automatically Check whether automatic opening is disabled. Call open_dashboard() or obtain the address with EpicycleIO.dashboard_url() and open it manually.\nA panel remains blank Check that the browser can load the required external libraries and, for trajectory views, map imagery.\nThe dashboard reports that it is disconnected Check that Julia is still running and the local server has not been stopped. A new plotting call restarts a stopped server.\nA panel is missing from a selected view Check the panel name and the names passed to open_dashboard. Use open_dashboard() to view all panels.\nEarlier data disappear after a plotting call Use the adding form, such as xyplot!, to retain existing traces.\nTraces multiply when a script is rerun Start the panel with a replacing call or use clear! before adding traces.\nAxis labels or legend settings do not change Apply layout settings with panel! and check the panel name.\n\nIf EpicycleIO cannot launch the browser, it issues a warning containing the local dashboard URL. Open that address manually on the machine running Julia.\n\nSee Data plotting, 3D plotting, and the API reference for more detail.","title":"Browser requirements and troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"#EpicycleIO","page":"Home","text":"EpicycleIO provides tools for visualizing and exporting Epicycle results. Use it to plot data, animate a spacecraft trajectory, write tabular reports, and exchange ephemeris files with other software.\n\nPlots and animated trajectories appear together in a browser dashboard served from your machine. The dashboard updates as your Julia session produces results, so you can inspect a propagation or follow a solver while it runs.\n\nTask Function Input\nPlot data xyplot and other plotting functions Arrays of values\nAnimate a trajectory orbitview A spacecraft with a recorded propagation history\nWrite a tabular report report Named columns of values\nRead an ephemeris read_ephemeris A CCSDS OEM, STK .e, or JPL SPK file\nExport a trajectory write_ephemeris A spacecraft or spacecraft history\n\nPlotting and reporting work with arrays, including data from sources other than Epicycle. For spacecraft results, use history and Calc to extract the quantities you need. Trajectory views and ephemeris functions work directly with spacecraft or history objects so they can use the associated epochs and coordinate systems.","title":"EpicycleIO"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"EpicycleIO is distributed through the Gen Astro registry under the Gen Astro Source Available License. Add the registry, then install the package:\n\nusing Pkg\npkg\"registry add General https://github.com/GenAstro/GenAstroRegistry.git\"\npkg\"add EpicycleIO\"\n\nThe registry command includes General because EpicycleIO's dependencies are registered there. You only need to add the registries once for a Julia installation.\n\nLoad EpicycleIO to plot arrays or write reports. The spacecraft examples on this page also use the Epicycle umbrella package, which must be installed in your active Julia environment. Epicycle re-exports EpicycleIO's public functions.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start:-plot-data","page":"Home","text":"Create a named panel with xyplot, then set its axis labels with panel!:\n\nusing EpicycleIO\n\nhours = collect(0.0:0.25:12.0)\naltitude_km = 400.0 .+ 25.0 .* sin.(hours)\n\nxyplot(\"Altitude\", hours, altitude_km; name = \"Spacecraft A\")\npanel!(\"Altitude\"; xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\", yaxis_title = \"Altitude (km)\")\n\nThe first plot opens the dashboard in your browser. Subsequent plots update the same dashboard. A leading string selects the panel; plotting again with the same name replaces that panel's plotted data. Use the ! form to add a series:\n\nxyplot!(\"Altitude\", hours, altitude_km .+ 30; name = \"Spacecraft B\")\n\nTrace options such as name, mode, and line_color follow Plotly conventions. Use panel! for layout options such as titles, axis labels, and ranges. Nested options use underscores: yaxis_title sets Plotly's yaxis.title property.\n\nA matrix draws one series per column, and a vector of vectors draws one series per component. See Data plotting for array shapes, trace types, styling, and subplot layouts.","title":"Quick start: plot data"},{"category":"section","location":"#Work-with-a-propagated-trajectory","page":"Home","text":"The following example propagates an Earth-orbiting spacecraft for one hour. It then displays the trajectory and plots the recorded distance from Earth's center. Initial position and velocity components are in km and km/s.\n\nusing Epicycle\n\nsat = Spacecraft(\n    state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0.0, 1300.0, 0.0, 7.35, 1.0]),\n    time = Time(\"2026-10-21T00:00:00\", UTC(), ISOT()),\n    name = \"Explorer\",\n)\n\nprop = OrbitPropagator(\n    ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())),\n    IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12),\n)\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationSeconds(), 3600.0))\n\norbitview(\"Trajectory\", sat)\n\ntimes, radius_km = history(Calc(epoch, sat), Calc(position_magnitude, sat))\nhours_from_epoch = [(t - first(times)) * 24 for t in times]\n\nxyplot(\"Radius\", hours_from_epoch, radius_km; name = \"Explorer\")\npanel!(\"Radius\"; xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\", yaxis_title = \"Radius (km)\")\n\nSubtracting two Time values gives an interval in days; multiplying by 24 produces the elapsed hours used on the horizontal axis.\n\norbitview reads the trajectory, epochs, and reference frame from the spacecraft's history. Its playback controls let you animate the orbit, and impulsive maneuvers are marked with their velocity changes. Earth is currently the only supported central body, and each trajectory panel holds one spacecraft. See 3D plotting for playback and display options.","title":"Work with a propagated trajectory"},{"category":"section","location":"#Export-results","page":"Home","text":"Choose a tabular report when you want columns of calculated quantities. Choose an ephemeris when you want a trajectory with its epochs, coordinate system, and interpolation information.","title":"Export results"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tabular-reports","page":"Home","text":"report writes one column for each keyword. All columns must have the same number of rows. Include units in the column names, and convert epochs to the representation you want readers to use.\n\noutput_dir = mktempdir()\nreport_path = joinpath(output_dir, \"radius.txt\")\nreport(report_path; hours_from_epoch = hours_from_epoch, radius_km = radius_km)\n\"/tmp/jl_QTT84m/radius.txt\"\n\nAn existing file is overwritten. Vector-valued columns expand into component columns, such as position_1, position_2, and position_3. For absolute epochs, convert Time values explicitly, for example with t.isot or t.jd, and identify the time system in the column heading. See Reporting for formatting and column requirements.","title":"Tabular reports"},{"category":"section","location":"#Ephemeris-files","page":"Home","text":"Write the spacecraft's recorded history, then read it back as a SpacecraftHistory:\n\nephemeris_path = joinpath(output_dir, \"explorer.oem\")\nwrite_ephemeris(ephemeris_path, sat; step = 60.0)\n\neph = read_ephemeris(ephemeris_path)\ncoverage(eph)\n1-element Vector{Tuple{Time, Time}}:\n (AstroEpochs.Time\n  value  = 2.4613345e6\n  scale  = UTC()\n  format = JD()\n, AstroEpochs.Time\n  value  = 2.4613345416666665e6\n  scale  = UTC()\n  format = JD()\n)\n\nThe extension selects CCSDS OEM (.oem), STK ephemeris (.e), or JPL SPK (.bsp); SPK also needs the object's naif_id. The writer's step specifies the output sampling interval in seconds. Without it, the writer keeps the source epochs. An existing output file is replaced.\n\nUse state_at to evaluate a loaded history within its coverage. You can also pass the history to write_ephemeris to convert it to another supported format. See Ephemeris files for examples, format requirements, metadata, interpolation, and diagnostics.","title":"Ephemeris files"},{"category":"section","location":"#Manage-the-dashboard","page":"Home","text":"Named panels let you keep related plots and trajectory views together. Use open_dashboard to show selected panels, and clear panels when you no longer need their contents:\n\nopen_dashboard(\"Altitude\", \"Radius\")\nclear!(\"Altitude\")  # Remove the contents of one panel.\nclear_all!()        # Remove all panels.\n\nFor scripts or automated runs, disable automatic browser opening before plotting:\n\nEpicycleIO.auto_open!(false)\n\nPlots use Plotly.js, and trajectory views use CesiumJS. The browser loads these libraries from external servers, so an internet connection is needed if they are not already cached. The dashboard itself runs locally and does not require an account or API key. See The dashboard for panel layout, live updates, and session controls.","title":"Manage the dashboard"},{"category":"section","location":"#Guides-and-reference","page":"Home","text":"Data plotting: array shapes, plot types, styling, and subplots.\n3D plotting: trajectory animation and display options.\nThe dashboard: panels, browser views, and live updates.\nReporting: column names, units, epoch formatting, and text output.\nEphemeris files: reading, evaluating, comparing, and exporting trajectories.\nAPI reference: function signatures, keywords, and detailed behavior.\n\nIn the Julia REPL, enter help mode with ?, then type a function name such as xyplot, orbitview, or write_ephemeris to read its documentation.","title":"Guides and reference"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#3D-Plotting","page":"3D plotting","text":"EpicycleIO displays animated spacecraft trajectories and three-dimensional data plots in the browser dashboard. Choose the function that matches your input:\n\nFunction Use Input\norbitview An animated trajectory around Earth A spacecraft with propagated history\nscatter3d Points or lines in three data axes Three equal-length coordinate arrays\nsurface A scalar field drawn as a surface A matrix, optionally with coordinate arrays\n\nThe first plotting call opens the dashboard automatically. See The dashboard for panel layout and browser requirements.","title":"3D Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Display-a-spacecraft-trajectory","page":"3D plotting","text":"Use orbitview after propagating a spacecraft. The view reads positions and times from its recorded history and uses the spacecraft's name as its label. There is no need to extract position arrays or supply a separate epoch.\n\nThis example propagates a low Earth orbit for six hours:\n\nusing Epicycle\n\nsat = Spacecraft(\n    state = CartesianState([7000.0, 0.0, 1300.0, 0.0, 7.35, 1.0]),\n    time = Time(\"2020-01-01T00:00:00.000\", UTC(), ISOT()),\n    name = \"Explorer\",\n)\n\nprop = OrbitPropagator(\n    ForceModel(PointMassGravity(earth, ())),\n    IntegratorConfig(DP8(); abstol = 1e-12, reltol = 1e-12, dt = 60.0),\n)\n\npropagate!(prop, sat, StopAt(sat, PropDurationDays(), 0.25))\norbitview(\"Trajectory\", sat)\n\nThe Cartesian state contains position in kilometers followed by velocity in kilometers per second. Each recorded propagation segment appears as a separate colored arc, with the spacecraft animated along the trajectory.\n\nA leading string selects the panel. Without it, orbitview(sat) uses the default panel, \"Plot\". Calling orbitview again with the same panel name replaces its contents and resets the displayed time and camera framing. Call it again after further propagation to display the updated history.","title":"Display a spacecraft trajectory"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Frames-and-time","page":"3D plotting","text":"The camera follows an inertial frame, so Earth rotates beneath the trajectory during playback. Recorded times are converted to UTC for display. Histories containing different time scales are supported.\n\nEarth is currently the only supported central body. The viewer treats recorded positions as Earth-centered inertial coordinates and converts kilometers to meters for the browser. It does not transform positions from the history's coordinate system. Use an Earth-centered inertial history; positions recorded in a body-fixed frame or another coordinate system require conversion before viewing.","title":"Frames and time"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Playback-controls","page":"3D plotting","text":"The controls beneath each view let you:\n\nPlay / pause the animation.\nReset the time to the start of the recorded trajectory.\nRun the animation slower or faster.\nToggle the stars background.\n\nThe time readout shows the current UTC epoch. Each trajectory panel has its own clock and playback controls.\n\nBy default, speed = :auto chooses a rate that plays the recorded time span in about two minutes. To set an initial rate, supply the number of simulated seconds per real second:\n\norbitview(\"Trajectory\", sat; speed = 3600)  # One simulated hour per real second.\n\nPlayback rates in the browser are limited to between 1 and 86,400 simulated seconds per real second. Automatic playback uses the same limits, so very short or very long trajectories may take a different amount of time to play.","title":"Playback controls"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Arc-colors-and-width","page":"3D plotting","text":"The default arc colors use the same palette as the data plots. Use colors to supply a different palette and linewidth to set the arc width in pixels:\n\norbitview(\"Trajectory\", sat;\n          colors = [(55, 200, 255, 255), (255, 165, 0, 255)],\n          linewidth = 2.5,\n          speed = 600)\n\nEach color is an (r, g, b, a) tuple of integers from 0 to 255; an alpha value of 255 is opaque. Colors follow propagation-segment order and cycle when there are more arcs than colors. A single tuple, such as colors = (55, 200, 255, 255), applies one color to every arc. The default line width is 1.5 pixels.","title":"Arc colors and width"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Maneuver-markers","page":"3D plotting","text":"When the history contains a single-sample impulsive-maneuver marker between propagation arcs, the view marks the burn and labels it with its name and ΔV magnitude in kilometers per second. The ΔV comes from the recorded velocity change across the marker; no separate maneuver list is needed.\n\nA velocity discontinuity without a recorded marker does not produce a burn label. Markers without a preceding propagation arc, or with a velocity change below 1 mm/s, are omitted.","title":"Maneuver markers"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Show-a-trajectory-beside-its-data","page":"3D plotting","text":"Use separate named panels to display an orbit view beside plots from the same run. Continuing the spacecraft example above:\n\nt, r = history(Calc(epoch, sat), Calc(position_vector, sat, EarthMJ2000Eq))\nhours = [(epoch_i - first(t)) * 24 for epoch_i in t]\nradius_km = [sqrt(sum(abs2, position_i)) for position_i in r]\n\nxyplot(\"Radius\", hours, radius_km; name = \"|r|\")\npanel!(\"Radius\";\n       xaxis_title = \"Hours from epoch\",\n       yaxis_title = \"Radius (km)\")\n\nopen_dashboard(\"Trajectory\", \"Radius\")\n\nThe radius plot provides a quantitative view of the same history. Its horizontal axis does not move with the orbit view's playback clock.\n\nEach orbit view currently holds one spacecraft. orbitview! is not implemented and raises an ArgumentError. To compare spacecraft, give each its own panel:\n\n# sat1 and sat2 must each have propagated history.\norbitview(\"Chaser\", sat1)\norbitview(\"Target\", sat2)\nopen_dashboard(\"Chaser\", \"Target\")","title":"Show a trajectory beside its data"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Plot-points-and-lines-in-three-axes","page":"3D plotting","text":"Use scatter3d for a three-dimensional data plot. It takes one x, y, and z value per point and uses the supplied values directly, without interpreting units, reference frames, or epochs.\n\nusing EpicycleIO\n\nangle = collect(range(0.0, 4π; length = 200))\nx = cos.(angle)\ny = sin.(angle)\nz = angle ./ (2π)\n\nscatter3d(\"Helix\", x, y, z;\n          mode = \"lines\", name = \"Curve\", line_color = \"cyan\")\npanel!(\"Helix\";\n       scene_xaxis_title = \"x\",\n       scene_yaxis_title = \"y\",\n       scene_zaxis_title = \"Turns\",\n       scene_aspectmode = \"data\")\n\nscatter3d!(\"Helix\", [first(x)], [first(y)], [first(z)];\n           mode = \"markers\", name = \"Start\", marker_size = 5)\n\nChoose mode = \"markers\", \"lines\", or \"lines+markers\" for the display. scatter3d replaces a panel's plotted data; scatter3d! adds a trace. Axis layout properties begin with scene_. For coordinates with the same units, scene_aspectmode = \"data\" preserves their relative scale.","title":"Plot points and lines in three axes"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Plot-a-surface","page":"3D plotting","text":"Use surface for values on a rectangular grid. Rows of Z correspond to y, and columns correspond to x, so size(Z) must equal (length(y), length(x)).\n\nusing EpicycleIO\n\nx = collect(range(-2.0, 2.0; length = 41))\ny = collect(range(-1.0, 1.0; length = 21))\nZ = [xi^2 + 2yi^2 for yi in y, xi in x]\n\nsurface(\"Cost\", x, y, Z; colorscale = \"Viridis\", colorbar_title = \"Cost\")\npanel!(\"Cost\";\n       scene_xaxis_title = \"x\",\n       scene_yaxis_title = \"y\",\n       scene_zaxis_title = \"Cost\")\n\nWith surface(\"Cost\", Z), the coordinate axes use the matrix indices. surface! adds a surface to an existing 3D data panel.\n\nKeep orbit views and data plots in separate panels. An orbit view uses a globe renderer, while scatter3d and surface use Plotly's three-axis scenes. Plotly layout settings supplied through panel! do not configure the orbit viewer.","title":"Plot a surface"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting_3d/#Troubleshooting","page":"3D plotting","text":"Symptom What to check\norbitview reports missing history Call propagate! before displaying the spacecraft. An initial state alone is insufficient.\nThe history has no drawable arcs At least one propagation segment must contain two or more samples. Empty segments and single-sample markers cannot form an arc.\nThe trajectory has an unexpected orientation or location Check that recorded positions are Earth-centered inertial coordinates in kilometers. The view does not transform other frames.\nA maneuver has no label Check that the history records a single-sample marker and a measurable velocity change.\nThe globe panel remains blank Check that the browser can load CesiumJS and Earth imagery. See The dashboard for network requirements.\n\nSee the API reference for function signatures and Data plotting for other plot types and trace attributes.","title":"Troubleshooting"}]
}
